# operator-path-literals.manifest — the CHECKED boundary for
# your-org/nexus-code#1275 (and the class #1174 opened).
#
# Generated by:  bash monitor/watcher/operator-path-literals.sh
# Enforced by:   bash monitor/watcher/test-operator-path-literals-manifest.sh
#
# One row per (file, family) with a COUNT. Keyed on counts, NOT line numbers:
# a line number is a count's twin and a stale one points at different text that
# still reads coherently (your-org/nexus-code#1163), so an edit above a
# recorded literal must not move this file.
#
# REGENERATING THIS FILE IS NOT A FIX — see the classifier header. A new row,
# or a count that went UP, means a doc or template gained an operator-specific
# absolute path. Ask which kind it is:
#
#   * a PER-READER path — their nexus root, their dotfile, their home. That is
#     the #1275 defect and the consequence is an ABSENCE: pasted onto another
#     host the line silently arms nothing, and the reader's evidence that it
#     worked is that their shell started normally. Write the general form
#     (`<YOUR_NEXUS_ROOT>`, `$USER`) and do NOT record it here.
#   * a SHARED resource at a fixed location every reader really does hit
#     literally. Record it, and put the reason in this header.
#
# A count that went DOWN is good; regenerate.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY EACH ROW BELOW IS LEGITIMATE — judged once, here, so the scan needs no
# semantic judgement at run time
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# docs/admin/your-org-addendum.md            fh-user  2   (:45, :51)
#   `barnacle` is a SHARED lab tool at a FIXED location on `/shared`, run by
#   full path and aliased. Every your-lab reader hits that exact path; it is
#   not a per-reader substitution. The file scopes itself in its own header —
#   *"Deployment-specific — removable … nothing here generalises"* — and the
#   SAME FILE demonstrates it understands the distinction: `:155` writes
#   `/shared/your-lab-m/user/$USER/nexus`, the correct general form, for the
#   thing that IS per-reader. The classifier excludes `$`-bearing segments, so
#   `:155` is correctly absent from this count.
#
# docs/agent-channel-rfc.md                  fh-user  3   (:1514, :1678, :1692)
#   Worked-example output and one sample command in an RFC. The surrounding
#   values are transparently illustrative (`foo-summary`, `alice`,
#   `your-org/your-nexus#412`), so the reader is being shown a shape, not told
#   to install a line.
#
# docs/reference/dependency-surface.md       fh-user  1   (:204)
#   The literal is an INPUT to a documented transformation — the project-slug
#   encoding `/shared/…/nexus` -> `-shared-your-lab-m-user-your-nexus`. Replacing it
#   with a placeholder would destroy the worked example.
#
# monitor/README.md                          home-user 1  (:1943)
#   Prose naming where the `agent-sandbox` component is installed on the
#   reporting host. Descriptive, inside a bulleted component list, with no
#   instruction to copy it. Recorded rather than rewritten because a component
#   inventory that named no concrete location would say less; if this file ever
#   gains an INSTALLABLE `/home/…` line the count goes to 2 and the guard is
#   red, which is the property being bought.
#
# skills/nexus.report/SKILL.md               fh-user  1   (:32)
#   Explicitly parenthesised as an example — *"(e.g. `…/reports`)"* — in a
#   sentence whose whole point is that the real value comes from the agent's
#   own `## Worker environment` header.
#
# skills/nexus.window-cleanup/SKILL.md       fh-user  1   (:825)
#   MEASUREMENT PROVENANCE: it names where a quoted count (12,379 entries) was
#   taken. A count is a property of a tree and naming that tree is required,
#   not incidental — stripping the path would make the number uncheckable,
#   which is the defect the repo files under "a count is a property of a tree".
#
